# Longest Substring with K Distinct Characters — GFG

**Pattern:** Variable-Size Sliding Window + HashMap

**Approach:** Expand the window by adding characters and tracking their frequencies in a `HashMap`. If the window contains more than `k` distinct characters, shrink it from the left. Update the maximum length only when the window contains exactly `k` distinct characters.

## Java Code
```java
class Solution {
    public int longestKSubstr(String s, int k) {
        int left = 0;
        HashMap<Character, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        int maxlen = -1;

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char ch = s.charAt(i);
            map.put(ch, map.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);

            while (map.size() > k) {
                char leftchar = s.charAt(left);
                map.put(leftchar, map.get(leftchar) - 1);

                if (map.get(leftchar) == 0) {
                    map.remove(leftchar);
                }

                left++;
            }

            if (map.size() == k) {
                maxlen = Math.max(maxlen, i - left + 1);
            }
        }

        return maxlen;
    }
}
```

## Dry Run
**Input:** `s = "aabacbebebe", k = 3`

- Expand the window and count each character's frequency.
- Shrink the window whenever distinct characters exceed `3`.
- Track the longest window containing exactly `3` distinct characters.

**Output:** `7`

Longest valid substring: `"cbebebe"`.

## Complexity
- **Time:** \(O(n)\) — each pointer moves forward at most `n` times.
- **Space:** \(O(k)\) — the map stores at most `k + 1` distinct characters temporarily.

## Key Takeaways
- `map.size()` gives the number of distinct characters.
- Store **frequencies**, not just whether a character exists.
- Remove a character from the map when its frequency becomes `0`.
- Update `maxlen` only when `map.size() == k`.
- Initialize `maxlen = -1` because the problem requires `-1` when no valid substring exists.
